---
import MainLayout from '../../layouts/MainLayout.astro';

const FUNCTIONS = [
  ['IF(condition, whenTrue, whenFalse)', 'branches', 'Only the taken branch is evaluated, so IF can guard against nulls and division by zero.'],
  ['ISBLANK(field)', 'boolean', 'True when null or empty string.'],
  ['ISNULL(field)', 'boolean', 'True when null.'],
  ['CONTAINS(field, "text")', 'boolean', 'Substring test.'],
  ['REGEX(field, "pattern")', 'boolean', 'Pattern test. Patterns over 200 characters are rejected.'],
  ['LENGTH(field)', 'number', 'Character count. Null counts as 0.'],
  ['ABS(number)', 'number', 'Absolute value.'],
  ['ROUND(number, decimals)', 'number', 'Round to N decimal places.'],
  ['YEAR(date) · MONTH(date) · DAY(date)', 'number', 'Date parts. MONTH is 1–12.'],
  ['DAYS_BETWEEN(from, to)', 'number', 'Whole days between two dates; null if either is missing.'],
  ['DAYS_UNTIL(date)', 'number', 'Days from today, negative once past; null with no date.'],
  ['UPPER(field) · LOWER(field) · TRIM(field)', 'text', 'Case and whitespace.'],
  ['TODAY()', 'date', 'Today at midnight.'],
  ['NOW()', 'date', 'Current date and time.'],
  ['DATEVALUE(field)', 'date', 'Coerce a text or number value to a date.'],
];

const TYPES = [
  ['number', 'A plain number — counts, quantities, day differences.'],
  ['currency', 'A number rendered with the currency symbol.'],
  ['percent', 'A number rendered with a % suffix.'],
  ['text', 'Text — joined names, labels, formatted strings.'],
  ['date', 'A date value.'],
  ['boolean', 'True or false, rendered as a checkbox.'],
];

const examples = `ROUND(quantity * unit_price, 2)            → currency
TRIM(first_name + " " + last_name)        → text
DAYS_UNTIL(closing_date)                  → number
IF(ISBLANK(nickname), first_name, nickname)  → text
IF(amount > 0, ROUND(discount / amount * 100, 1), 0)  → percent`;

const validateCall = `POST /api/v1/records/validate-formula
{
  "objectApiName": "deal_line_item",
  "expression": "ROUND(quantity * unit_price, 2)",
  "returnType": "currency"
}

→ { "success": true, "data": { "valid": true, "returnType": "number" } }`;

const mismatch = `{
  "valid": false,
  "error": "This formula returns text, but the field's return type is currency. Either change the return type to text, or adjust the formula to return currency.",
  "returnType": "text"
}`;
---

<MainLayout
  title="Formula Fields"
  description="Expression syntax, functions, return types and evaluation order for formula fields."
>
  <h1>Formula Fields</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    A formula field computes its value from other fields on the same record. The
    value is calculated and stored on every save, so list views, filters and sorts
    read it straight from the record.
  </p>

  <h2>Return types</h2>
  <p>
    Every formula field declares a return type. It decides how the computed value
    is <strong>stored</strong> and <strong>rendered</strong>, and which functions
    and examples the formula editor offers.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Return type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {TYPES.map(([t, meaning]) => (
          <tr><td><code>{t}</code></td><td>{meaning}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <strong>The declared type is enforced.</strong> On save, the expression's return
    type is inferred and compared against what you declared; a definite mismatch is
    rejected. <code>currency</code>, <code>percent</code> and <code>number</code> are
    interchangeable at this level — they are all numbers and differ only in
    presentation.
  </div>

  <p>
    Where the type can't be pinned down — a reference to a field that doesn't
    exist, or an <code>IF</code> whose branches return different kinds — inference
    yields <em>unknown</em> and the check passes. Rejecting on a guess would block
    legitimate formulas.
  </p>

  <h2>Syntax</h2>
  <p>
    Expressions reference fields by API name. Arithmetic
    (<code>+ - * /</code>), comparison (<code>== != &gt; &gt;= &lt; &lt;=</code>)
    and logic (<code>AND OR NOT</code>, or <code>&amp;&amp; || !</code>) all work.
    Strings take double or single quotes.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{examples}</code></pre>

  <div class="note">
    <strong><code>+</code> is overloaded.</strong> If either side is text it
    concatenates; otherwise it adds. So <code>amount + 1</code> is a number but
    <code>name + amount</code> is text — which is also how the return type is
    inferred.
  </div>

  <h2>Functions</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Returns</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {FUNCTIONS.map(([sig, returns, notes]) => (
          <tr>
            <td><code>{sig}</code></td>
            <td><code>{returns}</code></td>
            <td>{notes}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>Coalescing</h3>
  <p>
    <code>&amp;&amp;</code> and <code>||</code> return the operand rather than a
    boolean, so <code>nickname || first_name</code> is a text fallback — the same
    idiom as JavaScript.
  </p>

  <h2>Evaluation order</h2>
  <p>On save, values are produced in this order:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Field validation</strong> — types, required, uniqueness.</li>
    <li><strong>Validation rules</strong> — evaluated against the incoming data
      <em>plus</em> freshly computed formula values, so a rule can reference a formula.</li>
    <li><strong>Auto-numbers</strong> — allocated atomically.</li>
    <li><strong>Rollup summaries</strong> — aggregated from child records.</li>
    <li><strong>Formulas</strong> — computed last, so they can read rollups.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="warning">
    <strong>Formulas are evaluated in a single pass, in field order.</strong> A
    formula that reads another formula field is not guaranteed to see the other's
    fresh value in the same save. Where one calculation feeds another, prefer a
    single combined expression, or a hidden helper field that doesn't itself depend
    on a formula.
  </div>

  <h2>Nulls and errors</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A missing field reference evaluates to <code>null</code>.</li>
    <li>Arithmetic on <code>null</code> coerces it to <code>0</code>. Guard with
      <code>IF(ISBLANK(x), …)</code> when zero is the wrong answer.</li>
    <li>Division by zero raises an error; the field is stored as
      <code>null</code> and the failure is logged. Guard it:
      <code>IF(qty &gt; 0, total / qty, 0)</code>.</li>
    <li>Expressions are capped at 1000 characters, 24 levels of nesting and
      10,000 evaluation steps.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Checking an expression</h2>
  <p>
    <code>POST /records/validate-formula</code> parses and dry-runs an expression
    against the object's fields, and reports the inferred return type:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{validateCall}</code></pre>
  <p>A type mismatch comes back with an actionable message:</p>
  <pre><code>{mismatch}</code></pre>

  <h2>Constraints</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A formula cannot reference itself.</li>
    <li>Formula values are read-only over the API — sending one is ignored.</li>
    <li>Formula fields are excluded from CSV export.</li>
    <li>The expression evaluator has no <code>eval</code>: it is a
      recursive-descent parser and tree-walking interpreter, and only whitelisted
      functions and string methods can be called.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Safety</h2>
  <p>
    Only these string methods are callable on a field value:
    <code>includes</code>, <code>startsWith</code>, <code>endsWith</code>,
    <code>toLowerCase</code>, <code>toUpperCase</code>, <code>trim</code>. The only
    readable property is <code>length</code>. Anything else raises an error rather
    than reaching into the runtime.
  </p>
</MainLayout>
